<BaseLayout title="Retailer EDI onboarding checklist for suppliers | EDI Spark" description="Prepare for retailer EDI onboarding: gather requirements, agree document flows, assign responsibilities, test and plan your first live exchange.">
  <SiteHeader current="/guides" />
  <main id="main-content" class="guide-page guide-article">
    <nav class="guide-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/guides">Supplier guides</a><span aria-hidden="true"> / </span><span>Retailer onboarding</span></nav>
    <header class="guide-intro"><p class="guide-eyebrow">Supplier checklist</p><h1>Prepare for your first retailer EDI exchange.</h1><p>Getting an EDI account and getting a retailer’s approval are different milestones. Agree the document requirements, responsibilities and acceptance tests before setting a go-live date.</p><p class="guide-date">EDI Spark editorial · Updated <time datetime="2026-11-21">21 November 2026</time></p></header>
    <section><h2>1. Gather the retailer’s instructions</h2><p>Ask your retailer contact for the current supplier onboarding pack and the technical contact or service responsible for EDI. Keep the version and date with your project notes so everyone works from the same requirements.</p><ul><li>Your supplier reference and relevant company, product and delivery-location identifiers.</li><li>Required business documents, message specifications and sample files.</li><li>Connection or network instructions and testing requirements.</li><li>Any commercial deadline, delivery-labelling requirements and approval process.</li></ul><p>Do not send passwords or confidential live documents in an initial enquiry. Agree a suitable secure channel when technical access or test material is needed.</p></section>
    <section><h2>2. Agree the document journey</h2><p>Write down what should happen from order receipt to invoice delivery. For each step, record the source system, recipient and person responsible for dealing with a problem.</p><div class="guide-table-wrap"><table><caption>Illustrative workflow to confirm with your retailer</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Document</th><th scope="col">Typical question to resolve</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Purchase order</th><td>How will the order reach the team or system that fulfils it?</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Order response</th><td>Must you confirm acceptance or report quantity changes?</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Despatch advice</th><td>Which shipment, packaging and tracking details are required?</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Invoice</th><td>Which order references, tax details and totals must match?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>This is an example, not a statement that every retailer requires these documents or that a particular route supports them. Confirm the actual list and each direction of exchange.</p></section>
    <section><h2>3. Confirm the connection scope</h2><p>Tell your provider which business system you use, its version and how you work today. A manual workspace, a file import and an automated ERP integration have different implementation needs.</p><ul><li>Record whether the proposed route uses a native connector, a third-party automation tool or a custom API or file exchange.</li><li>Assign responsibility for mappings, access, product data and retailer communication.</li><li>Confirm implementation work, ongoing support, separate third-party charges and how changes are handled.</li><li>Agree what can be tested during a trial before committing to live operation.</li></ul><p>A name in a <a href="/partners">trading partner directory</a> or <a href="/integrations">system directory</a> is a starting point for assessment, not evidence that your exact workflow is ready.</p></section>
    <section><h2>4. Test the business outcome</h2><p>Agree test cases with the retailer and provider. Check that a document reaches the right destination and that the receiving team or system can use it correctly.</p><ul><li>Match identifiers, quantities, units, dates, references, tax and totals against expected values.</li><li>Include relevant exceptions, such as a missing reference, a changed order or a rejected invoice.</li><li>Check who sees errors, how they investigate them and what the agreed correction process is.</li><li>Keep evidence of accepted tests and obtain the retailer’s required approval before switching to live exchanges.</li></ul><p>Use the retailer’s actual acceptance criteria. Technical delivery alone does not establish that the retailer has approved the transaction or onboarding.</p></section>
    <section><h2>5. Plan the first live exchange</h2><p>Choose a cutover window, confirm who will be available and agree a fallback if the first transaction fails. Avoid sending the same business document through old and new routes without an agreed duplicate-handling process.</p><p>Follow the first live documents through receipt, processing and the required acknowledgements. Check that the operational team knows where to see status and who to contact. Retain the previous route only for the agreed transition period and with clear responsibilities.</p></section>
    <section><h2>What determines the timeline?</h2><p>Retailer testing schedules, access to your business system, product-data quality and mapping work can all affect the plan. Ask for milestones for connection assessment, test readiness, retailer approval and first live exchange. A platform setup estimate should not be treated as a guaranteed retailer go-live date.</p></section>
    <aside class="guide-callout"><h2>Start with the retailer and system you use.</h2><p>Bring the onboarding instructions you already have and we’ll assess the route and information needed next.</p><a class="button" href="/contact?intent=connection">Discuss your connection</a></aside>
    <section class="guide-sources"><h2>How to use this checklist</h2><p>This is EDI Spark’s general planning checklist, not a retailer specification or confirmation of product capabilities. Your retailer’s current supplier instructions and your agreed implementation scope take precedence. Read <a href="/guides/edi-costs">how to estimate EDI costs</a> before comparing offers.</p></section>
  </main><SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
